Z||000002||fastback87||12-07-2001||11:13 PM||||It almost sounds like wheel bearing.  They aren't too bad to replace.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||12-07-2001||11:30 PM||||Could be dog bone bushing that holds the engine in place.  Any torque on the engine, it shifts and clunks.  Release of torque, the engine shifts and clunks.<BR>Or some other engine mount bracket.||||reg||1
Z||000004||bad911||12-07-2001||11:36 PM||||You're describing classic CV joint failure symptoms.<P>------------------<BR>87 2.8 AT SE<BR>85 4.9 project||||reg||1
Z||000005||Alex2004||12-07-2001||11:54 PM||||oh....forgot to say, i only hear the clunking noise whenever the brakes are applied when slowing down going forward...but in reverse it doesn't matter, i hear it w/ them applied or not....thanks!!!<P>Alex...||||reg||1
Z||000006||camon||12-08-2001||04:55 AM||||The rattle you hear in the rear wheel could be loose pads. Kick the tire and see if it makes the rattle noise. If it does jack it up and try to wiggle the tire......if it wiggles it's more then likely the wheel bearing if it don't wiggle I would have to say brake pads are loose.<P>Mine had a rear wheel rattle and my inner prake pad on the driverside was loose and rattling aginst the rotor!<P><BR>ohh yeah you can findout if it is the pads by driving over a bump that would normally make it rattle and lightly apply the brakes when going over the bump. If you don't hear the noise it is the pads...if it still does make the noise then I don't know!<P>------------------<BR>   Keith<BR>1987 Fiero GT<p>[This message has been edited by camon (edited 12-08-2001).]||||reg||3
